---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS): The Mathematical Engine of Trait Mapping";
const description = "A first-principles, rigorous exploration of Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS) — from genotype dosage matrices and OLS regression to population stratification PCA, Linkage Disequilibrium, and Polygenic Risk Scores.";
const readingTime = "18 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article" data-deep-dive-gwas>
    <!-- Back to Catalog -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Computational Genomics Deep Dives
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Article Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical & Population Genetics</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Foundational to Advanced</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      How do 3 billion base pairs of human DNA translate into phenotypic diversity, disease predisposition, and complex human traits? <strong>Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS)</strong> provide the foundational statistical and algorithmic bridge connecting population-scale genotype matrices to quantitative traits and clinical risk.
    </p>

    <!-- Callout: Executive Summary -->
    <div class="deep-dive-callout">
      <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
        <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Executive Summary & Core Mechanisms
      </div>
      <p>
        GWAS tests millions of genetic variants across cohorts of tens to hundreds of thousands of individuals using <strong>additive linear/logistic regression</strong>. To discover genuine biological signals, a GWAS must rigorously solve two major statistical challenges: <strong>population stratification confounding</strong> (via Ancestry PCA or Linear Mixed Models) and <strong>massive multiple testing</strong> (via the genome-wide Bonferroni threshold <Katex tex="p < 5 \times 10^{-8}" />).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section>
      <h2>1. The Genotype Dosage Matrix & Single-Variant Testing</h2>
      <p>
        At the core of every GWAS is a high-dimensional cohort dataset containing <Katex tex="N" /> individuals assayed across <Katex tex="M" /> single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs). For an individual <Katex tex="i" /> and variant <Katex tex="j" />, genotype data is encoded as an <strong>additive dosage</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Additive Genotype Dosage Encoding</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\} \quad \longrightarrow \quad \text{Count of Minor (Effect) Alleles}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="0 = \text{Ref/Ref (Homozygous reference)}" /> &emsp;|&emsp; 
          <Katex tex="1 = \text{Ref/Alt (Heterozygous)}" /> &emsp;|&emsp; 
          <Katex tex="2 = \text{Alt/Alt (Homozygous alternative)}" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        While the <strong>additive model</strong> is the standard assumption in human genetics due to parsimony and power, alternative genetic architectures can also be tested:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Additive Model:</strong> <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /> (each minor allele linearly shifts phenotype by <Katex tex="\beta_j" />).</li>
        <li><strong>Dominant Model:</strong> <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 1\}" /> (one copy is sufficient to confer full phenotypic effect).</li>
        <li><strong>Recessive Model:</strong> <Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 0, 1\}" /> (requires two copies of the minor allele).</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Quantitative Traits: Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) Regression</h3>
      <p>
        For a continuous quantitative phenotype <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} \in \mathbb{R}^N" /> (e.g. standing height, LDL cholesterol, or fasting glucose), we formulate a single-variant Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression model:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Single-Variant Linear Regression Model</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="y_i = \alpha + \beta_j X_{ij} + \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma_k Z_{ik} + \epsilon_i, \quad \epsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="X_{ij}" /> = Additive genotype dosage &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\beta_j" /> = Effect size per minor allele &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="Z_{ik}" /> = Covariates (Age, Sex, Ancestry PCs)
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Using the Frisch-Waugh-Lovell theorem to project out the covariate matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{Z}" />, the analytical Ordinary Least Squares estimate of the effect size <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_j" /> and its variance are given by:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">OLS Estimator, Standard Error & Statistical Power</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_j = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^N (X_{ij} - \bar{X}_j)(y_i - \bar{y})}{\sum_{i=1}^N (X_{ij} - \bar{X}_j)^2} = \frac{\text{Cov}(X_j, y)}{\text{Var}(X_j)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\text{Var}(\hat{\beta}_j) = \frac{\sigma^2}{2 N p_j (1 - p_j)} \implies \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j) = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{2 N p_j (1 - p_j)}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="t_j = \frac{\hat{\beta}_j}{\text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j)} \sim \text{Student's } t(df = N - K - 2) \quad \longrightarrow \quad p_j = 2 \cdot [1 - \Phi(|t_j|)]" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          Under Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium with minor allele frequency <Katex tex="p_j = \text{MAF}" />, the genotype variance is <Katex tex="\text{Var}(X_j) = 2p_j(1 - p_j)" />. Notice that discovery power is directly proportional to <Katex tex="\sqrt{N \cdot p_j (1 - p_j) \cdot \beta_j^2}" />!
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Case-Control Binary Traits: Logistic Regression & Odds Ratios</h3>
      <p>
        For binary clinical disease status <Katex tex="y_i \in \{0, 1\}" /> (e.g. Type 2 Diabetes case vs. control), we fit a Logistic Regression model via Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE):
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Logistic Regression & Odds Ratio (OR)</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\ln\left(\frac{P(y_i = 1)}{1 - P(y_i = 1)}\right) = \alpha + \beta_j X_{ij} + \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma_k Z_{ik}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\text{Odds Ratio (OR)} = e^{\beta_j}, \quad 95\% \text{ CI} = \left[ e^{\hat{\beta}_j - 1.96 \cdot \text{SE}}, \; e^{\hat{\beta}_j + 1.96 \cdot \text{SE}} \right]" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Embedded Interactive Widget 1: Regression Sandbox -->
      <div class="embedded-widget">
        <div class="widget-header">
          <span>Interactive Sandbox: Single-Variant OLS Regression</span>
          <span style="font-size: var(--fs-meta); color: var(--color-muted);">Live Reactive Simulation</span>
        </div>
        <div class="widget-body">
          <div class="widget-viewport">
            <svg class="gwas-regr-svg" data-dd-regr-svg aria-label="Interactive Regression Plot"></svg>
          </div>
          <div class="widget-controls">
            <div class="widget-slider-group">
              <label for="dd-beta">Effect Size (&beta;):</label>
              <input id="dd-beta" type="range" min="-0.6" max="0.6" step="0.05" value="0.35" data-dd-beta-slider />
              <span class="widget-readout" data-dd-beta-val>+0.35</span>
            </div>
            <div class="widget-slider-group">
              <label for="dd-maf">Minor Allele Freq (MAF):</label>
              <input id="dd-maf" type="range" min="0.05" max="0.5" step="0.05" value="0.25" data-dd-maf-slider />
              <span class="widget-readout" data-dd-maf-val>25%</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem; text-align: center; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8rem; color: var(--color-ink);" data-dd-regr-stats>
            Slope β̂ = 0.350 | SE = 0.042 | t = 8.33 | p = 1.20e-13
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section>
      <h2>2. The Population Stratification Conundrum ("The Chopsticks Gene")</h2>
      <p>
        The most notorious pitfall in statistical genetics is <strong>population stratification</strong>. In 1994, Eric Lander and Nicholas Schork described a classic thought experiment: imagine running a GWAS for <i>chopstick dexterity</i> in a mixed cohort of European and East Asian individuals living in San Francisco.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> The Chopsticks Problem & Spurious Association
        </div>
        <p>
          Chopstick usage is a cultural trait strongly correlated with East Asian ancestry. Simultaneously, thousands of completely unrelated alleles across the genome have divergent frequencies between Europe and East Asia due to historical genetic drift. A naive regression will find <strong>hundreds of thousands of false-positive "chopstick genes"</strong> across every chromosome!
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Mathematically, omitted ancestry <Katex tex="A" /> confounds the relationship because <Katex tex="\text{Cov}(X, A) \neq 0" /> and <Katex tex="\text{Cov}(y, A) \neq 0" />:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Mathematical Decomposition of Ancestry Confounding Bias</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\hat{\beta}_{\text{naive}}] = \beta_j + \frac{\text{Cov}(X_j, A)}{\text{Var}(X_j)} \cdot \gamma_{\text{Ancestry}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          When ancestry effect <Katex tex="\gamma_{\text{Ancestry}} \neq 0" />, the naive estimator is heavily biased away from 0 even if the true genetic effect <Katex tex="\beta_j = 0" />!
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Ancestry Principal Component Analysis (EIGENSTRAT)</h3>
      <p>
        To eliminate stratification, Price et al. (2006) introduced <strong>EIGENSTRAT</strong>. We construct the normalized genotype matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{G}^*" /> where each entry is standardized by its allele frequency <Katex tex="p_j" />:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Genotype Standardization & SVD Decomposition</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="G^*_{ij} = \frac{G_{ij} - 2p_j}{\sqrt{2p_j(1 - p_j)}} \quad \implies \quad \mathbf{G}^* = \mathbf{U} \mathbf{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^T" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{PC}_k = \mathbf{U}_k \cdot \sigma_k \quad (k = 1, \dots, 10)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          Top eigenvectors <Katex tex="\mathbf{PC}_1, \dots, \mathbf{PC}_{10}" /> capture continental and sub-continental ancestry axes. Including them as covariates in regression orthogonalizes the association test.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Linear Mixed Models (LMM: EMMAX / BOLT-LMM)</h3>
      <p>
        When cohorts contain cryptic relatedness, family structures, or complex population structure, <strong>Linear Mixed Models (LMM)</strong> replace fixed PC covariates with a random polygenic background effect:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Linear Mixed Model Formulation</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\beta + \mathbf{g}_j \gamma_j + \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{\epsilon}, \quad \mathbf{u} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_g^2 \mathbf{K}), \quad \mathbf{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_e^2 \mathbf{I})" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{K} = \frac{1}{M} \mathbf{G}^* (\mathbf{G}^*)^T \quad \text{(Empirical Genetic Relationship Matrix)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\sigma_g^2" /> = Genetic variance component &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\sigma_e^2" /> = Environmental variance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="h^2_{\text{SNP}} = \frac{\sigma_g^2}{\sigma_g^2 + \sigma_e^2}" /> (Narrow-sense SNP Heritability)
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Genomic Inflation Factor (&lambda;_GC) & Q-Q Plot Diagnostics</h3>
      <p>
        To audit whether a GWAS suffers from uncorrected confounding, we calculate the <strong>Genomic Inflation Factor (<Katex tex="\lambda_{GC}" />)</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Genomic Inflation Factor Formula</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\lambda_{GC} = \frac{\text{median}(\chi^2_{\text{obs}, 1}, \dots, \chi^2_{\text{obs}, M})}{0.454936}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="0.454936" /> is the theoretical median of a 1-degree-of-freedom Chi-Square distribution <Katex tex="\chi^2(df = 1)" /> under the null hypothesis. A pristine study yields <Katex tex="\lambda_{GC} \le 1.05" />.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Embedded Interactive Widget 2: PCA Confounding Simulator -->
      <div class="embedded-widget">
        <div class="widget-header">
          <span>Interactive Simulator: Population Stratification & Q-Q Inflation</span>
          <span style="font-size: var(--fs-meta); color: var(--color-muted);">PCA Covariates Comparison</span>
        </div>
        <div class="widget-body">
          <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 1rem;">
            <div>
              <div style="font-size: var(--fs-meta); font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.35rem; color: var(--color-ink);">1. Ancestry PCA (PC1 vs PC2)</div>
              <div class="widget-viewport" style="height: 220px;">
                <svg data-dd-pca-svg aria-label="Ancestry PCA Plot"></svg>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div style="font-size: var(--fs-meta); font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 0.35rem; color: var(--color-ink);">2. Q-Q Quantile Plot</div>
              <div class="widget-viewport" style="height: 220px;">
                <svg data-dd-qq-svg aria-label="Q-Q Plot"></svg>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="widget-controls" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            <label class="gwas-toggle-wrap">
              <input type="checkbox" class="gwas-toggle-input" data-dd-pca-toggle checked />
              <span>Include Ancestry PC Covariates</span>
            </label>
            <span class="widget-readout" data-dd-lambda-readout style="font-size: 0.85rem;">λ_GC = 1.01 (Controlled Null)</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section>
      <h2>3. Genome-Wide Multiple Testing & The Manhattan Plot</h2>
      <p>
        A typical GWAS evaluates between <Katex tex="1{,}000{,}000" /> and <Katex tex="10{,}000{,}000" /> imputed genetic variants simultaneously. If a standard statistical threshold of <Katex tex="\alpha = 0.05" /> were used, testing <Katex tex="10^6" /> null variants would produce <strong>50,000 false-positive discoveries</strong> purely by chance.
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Derivation of the Genome-Wide Significance Threshold</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\alpha_{\text{GWAS}} = \frac{\alpha_{\text{FWER}}}{M_{\text{eff}}} = \frac{0.05}{1{,}000{,}000} = 5 \times 10^{-8}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.4rem;">
          <Katex tex="-\log_{10}(5 \times 10^{-8}) \approx 7.301" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="M_{\text{eff}} \approx 10^6" /> represents the effective number of independent correlation blocks across the human genome due to Linkage Disequilibrium (LD).
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        To visualize all 22 autosomes simultaneously, GWAS results are plotted as a <strong>Manhattan Plot</strong>: genomic coordinates are aligned along the horizontal x-axis from Chromosome 1 to 22, while the vertical y-axis displays <Katex tex="-\log_{10}(P)" />. Strong association signals rise above the background noise like skyscrapers on the Manhattan skyline.
      </p>

      <!-- Embedded Interactive Widget 3: Manhattan Explorer -->
      <div class="embedded-widget">
        <div class="widget-header">
          <span>Interactive Explorer: Multi-Chromosome Manhattan Plot</span>
          <span style="font-size: var(--fs-meta); color: var(--color-muted);">Select Biological Trait Presets</span>
        </div>
        <div class="widget-body">
          <div style="display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.35rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
            <button type="button" class="gwas-preset-btn active" data-dd-preset="t2d">Type 2 Diabetes (TCF7L2)</button>
            <button type="button" class="gwas-preset-btn" data-dd-preset="ldl">LDL Cholesterol (PCSK9)</button>
            <button type="button" class="gwas-preset-btn" data-dd-preset="ad">Alzheimer's (APOE)</button>
            <button type="button" class="gwas-preset-btn" data-dd-preset="height">Height (Polygenic)</button>
          </div>
          <div class="widget-viewport" style="height: 240px;">
            <svg data-dd-manhattan-svg aria-label="Interactive Manhattan Plot"></svg>
          </div>
          <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem; padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem; background: var(--color-surface); border: 1px solid var(--color-rule); border-radius: 6px;" data-dd-lead-card>
            <strong>Lead Hit: rs7903146 (TCF7L2) — chr10:114,758,349</strong>
            <div style="font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--color-muted); margin-top: 0.2rem;">
              p = 1.20e-19 (-log₁₀ P = 18.92) | β = +0.340 | MAF = 30.0% | intron variant (enhancer)
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section>
      <h2>4. Linkage Disequilibrium (LD), Recombination & Fine-Mapping</h2>
      <p>
        A common misconception is that a GWAS skyscraper peak points to the exact single causal nucleotide. Because chromosomes are inherited in chunks during meiosis, alleles close to each other are transmitted together in <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium (LD)</strong> blocks.
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Linkage Disequilibrium Metrics (D, r², D')</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D = p_{AB} - p_A \cdot p_B, \quad r^2 = \frac{D^2}{p_A (1 - p_A) p_B (1 - p_B)}, \quad D' = \frac{D}{D_{\max}}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="r^2 = 1.0" /> represents complete correlation (perfect proxy SNPs). 
          <Katex tex="r^2 \ge 0.8" /> defines high-confidence LD candidate tags.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>LD Score Regression (LDSC): Polygenicity vs. Confounding</h3>
      <p>
        How do we distinguish whether a slightly elevated <Katex tex="\lambda_{GC} = 1.15" /> is caused by genuine polygenicity (thousands of real small-effect variants) or residual confounding? <strong>LD Score Regression (Bulik-Sullivan et al., 2015)</strong> exploits the fact that variants in high-LD regions tag more genetic variance:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">LD Score Regression Equation</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi^2_j] = 1 + \frac{N h^2_{\text{SNP}}}{M} \cdot \ell_j + N a" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\ell_j = \sum_k r^2_{jk}" /> is the LD score of SNP <Katex tex="j" />. The slope reveals true heritability <Katex tex="h^2_{\text{SNP}}" />, while the intercept <Katex tex="1 + Na" /> measures pure uncorrected confounding (<Katex tex="a = 0" /> under perfect control)!
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Statistical Fine-Mapping & 95% Credible Sets
        </div>
        <p>
          Methods like <strong>SuSiE (Sum of Single Effects)</strong> and <strong>PAINTOR</strong> use Bayesian posterior modeling to construct a <strong>95% Credible Set</strong> — the minimal set of variants that contains the true causal mutation with 95% certainty:
          <Katex tex="\sum_{j \in \text{CS}_{95\%}} \text{PIP}_j \ge 0.95" display={true} />
          Integrating functional epigenomics (eQTLs, ChIP-seq, OpenSpliceAI deep learning splice models) pinpoints the causal molecular mechanism.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section>
      <h2>5. Polygenic Risk Scores (PRS) & Clinical Translation</h2>
      <p>
        For complex polygenic conditions (e.g. coronary artery disease, breast cancer, type 2 diabetes), no single variant causes the disease. Instead, thousands of subtle variants across the genome contribute small additive increments to an individual's overall liability:
      </p>

      <div class="equation-card">
        <div class="equation-header">Polygenic Risk Score (PRS) Formulation</div>
        <div class="equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\text{PRS}_i = \sum_{j=1}^M \hat{\beta}_j \cdot G_{ij}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_j" /> = Marginal effect weight from discovery GWAS &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="G_{ij}" /> = Individual's dosage for variant <Katex tex="j" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Embedded Interactive Widget 4: PRS Liability Calculator -->
      <div class="embedded-widget">
        <div class="widget-header">
          <span>Interactive Calculator: Individual Polygenic Risk Distribution</span>
          <span style="font-size: var(--fs-meta); color: var(--color-muted);">Clinical Liability Stratification</span>
        </div>
        <div class="widget-body">
          <div class="widget-viewport" style="height: 220px;">
            <svg data-dd-prs-svg aria-label="PRS Bell Curve"></svg>
          </div>
          <div class="widget-controls">
            <div class="widget-slider-group">
              <label for="dd-prs">Individual PRS Percentile:</label>
              <input id="dd-prs" type="range" min="1" max="99" step="1" value="85" data-dd-prs-slider />
            </div>
            <span class="widget-readout" data-dd-prs-readout>85th Percentile &rarr; Odds Ratio OR = 1.95&times;</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-clinical">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="activity" size={15} /> Clinical Utility of Polygenic Risk Scores
        </div>
        <p>
          Individuals falling in the top <strong>5% of the PRS distribution</strong> often face disease risks equivalent to rare, monogenic high-penetrance mutations (such as <i>LDLR</i> in familial hypercholesterolemia or <i>BRCA1</i> in breast cancer). PRS enables personalized early intervention, lifestyle modifications, and proactive clinical screening decades before clinical symptom onset.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6: Summary & Hands-On Visualizer -->
    <section>
      <h2>6. Summary Cheat-Sheet & Key Invariants</h2>

      <div class="summary-table-wrap">
        <table class="summary-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>GWAS Component</th>
              <th>Mathematical Formulation</th>
              <th>Intuition & Purpose</th>
              <th>Diagnostic Benchmark</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Additive Dosage</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="G_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /></td>
              <td>Count of minor effect alleles</td>
              <td>MAF &ge; 1%, HWE p &gt; 10⁻⁶</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Association Test</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="t_j = \hat{\beta}_j / \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j)" /></td>
              <td>Evaluates <Katex tex="H_0: \beta_j = 0" /></td>
              <td>OLS for quantitative, Logistic for binary</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Ancestry PCA</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathbf{G}^* = \mathbf{U} \mathbf{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^T" /></td>
              <td>Decomposes continental ancestry</td>
              <td>Top 10–20 PCs as covariates</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Linear Mixed Model</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\beta + \mathbf{u} + \mathbf{\epsilon}" /></td>
              <td>Random polygenic effect with GRM <Katex tex="\mathbf{K}" /></td>
              <td>Estimates SNP heritability <Katex tex="h^2_{\text{SNP}}" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Genomic Inflation</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\lambda_{GC} = \frac{\text{median}(\chi^2)}{0.455}" /></td>
              <td>Measures confounding bias</td>
              <td>Controlled: <Katex tex="\lambda_{GC} \le 1.05" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Multiple Testing</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\alpha = \frac{0.05}{10^6} = 5 \times 10^{-8}" /></td>
              <td>Bonferroni correction for 10⁶ LD blocks</td>
              <td>Threshold: <Katex tex="p < 5 \times 10^{-8}" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Linkage Disequilibrium</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="r^2 = \frac{D^2}{p_A q_A p_B q_B}" /></td>
              <td>Measures allele co-inheritance</td>
              <td>Fine-mapping tag: <Katex tex="r^2 \ge 0.8" /></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Polygenic Score</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\text{PRS} = \sum \hat{\beta}_j \cdot G_j" /></td>
              <td>Aggregates genome-wide polygenic risk</td>
              <td>Clinical high risk: Top 5% quintile</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

